    "response": "Here is an objective assessment of what the all-male panel might signify and how you should approach it.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Does it indicate gender discrimination in your case?\n**Legally and practically, no.** \nBecause both you and the successful candidate are men, an all-male panel cannot reasonably be argued to have discriminated against you on the basis of sex. Raising the panel's gender composition as a personal grievance about your non-selection would likely be dismissed and could weaken your credibility.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is it a sign of anything broader?\nWhile it may not explain your non-selection relative to another man, an all-male selection panel at a public broadcaster is notable for several reasons:\n\n* **Potential breach of recruitment policy:** Most public broadcasters and public-sector organizations have strict Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion (EDI) policies requiring gender-balanced panels—especially for senior editorial roles. An all-male panel of four may indicate that standard HR procedures were bypassed or that exemptions were granted due to scheduling or internal politics.\n* **Organizational culture:** It can point to an insular leadership culture (\"old boys' network\") or a lack of women in senior editorial management at that station. \n\n---\n\n### 3. What is more likely behind the decision?\nBased on the details you shared, two other factors are far more pertinent:\n\n1. **The Strategic Priority: Digital Transformation vs. Traditional Hierarchy**\n   * Public broadcasters globally are under intense pressure to shift resources from traditional broadcast to digital, streaming, and mobile.\n   * Even though you have 15 years of experience (including 5 as deputy editor), the panel may have prioritized a candidate whose vision, portfolio, and background are geared toward digital audience growth, rather than traditional linear newsroom management.\n2. **Potential Age Bias (Implicit or Explicit)**\n   * While 8 years of experience is solid, it is roughly half your tenure. \n   * Panels sometimes harbor unconscious biases associating younger candidates with \"digital fluency\" and more experienced candidates with \"legacy systems.\" If there was any improper bias, age perception—framed around \"digital capability\"—is far more plausible than gender bias.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Next Steps & Recommendations\n\n* **Do not make an issue of the panel's gender composition right now:** \n  Bringing up the all-male panel in your request for feedback will likely distract from the substantive issues and make you appear defensive about a factor that did not directly disadvantage you against another male candidate.\n* **Politely follow up on your feedback request:**\n  Two weeks is standard (and often slow) in public institutions due to HR bureaucracy. Send a polite, constructive email to HR or the panel chair:\n  > *\"I am following up on my request of [date] regarding feedback on my interviews for the News Editor position. As I remain very committed to the organization, constructive feedback on my interview scoring and areas for future development would be immensely valuable.\"*\n* **Ask for the objective scoring matrix:**\n  Public broadcasters almost always use standardized competency-based interview scoring sheets. When you receive feedback, ask specifically where the scoring gap was between you and the successful candidate (e.g., editorial judgment, leadership, or digital strategy).\n* **If you suspect procedural irregularity later:**\n  If the written feedback indicates that non-job-related criteria were used, or if you believe internal recruitment rules were broken, you can review the broadcaster’s published recruitment and selection code of practice. Only if there is clear evidence of procedural unfairness should you consider escalating through internal HR or union channels.\n",
